---
permalink: /public/robots.txt
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#-
  robots.txt, written by the build rather than committed as a static file,
  because what it must say depends on which deploy this is.

  THE PERMALINK GOES THROUGH public/ ON PURPOSE, and /public/ is not part of
  the served path. The Vite plugin renames Eleventy's output to a temp folder
  and rebuilds with emptyOutDir, discarding anything it does not reference, and
  a plain robots.txt sitting in the output root is exactly that. Vite copies its
  publicDir verbatim to the output root, so this lands at /robots.txt. Same
  route _headers, _redirects and site.webmanifest take, and the same reason:
  passthrough alone silently lost all three. See DESIGN_SYSTEM.md, Deploy
  pipeline.

  Both halves of the indexing rule read deploy.indexable, so robots.txt and the
  meta tag in base.njk cannot disagree about the same deploy.

  WHY THE PREVIEW ALLOWS CRAWLING RATHER THAN DISALLOWING IT. It used to send
  Disallow: /, and that was the weaker of the two.

  Disallow prevents crawling, which prevents a crawler ever reading the noindex.
  That is correct only if the host has never been indexed. If it has, Disallow
  LOCKS THOSE URLS IN THE INDEX: Google cannot recrawl to discover the
  directive, so the entries persist as bare URLs with no snippet, and the
  instruction meant to remove them is the thing preventing their removal.

  Allow plus noindex prevents indexing just as effectively, because a crawler
  that fetches the page reads the directive and drops it, and it also clears an
  existing listing. It is correct in both states, which is why it is the one to
  use when the state is unknown.

  Whether this preview host is currently indexed has not been confirmed.

  So the preview's protection is the noindex meta tag on every page, and this
  file exists to let the crawler reach it.
-#}
{#-
  INTERNAL TOOLS ARE DISALLOWED ON EVERY DEPLOY, and that is not a
  contradiction of the reasoning above. That argument is about the preview HOST,
  which serves pages that exist on production and may already be indexed, so
  Disallow there would hide the noindex that has to be read to clear them.

  Neither applies to /yelp-map/. It has never been linked from anywhere, never
  submitted, and never indexed, so there is no listing for a crawler to need to
  reach a directive about. The noindex meta tag on the page is still the real
  mechanism; this is the second layer, and it is the one that keeps the URL out
  of a crawl even if it is guessed.
-#}
{%- set internalOnly = ["/yelp-map/"] -%}
# robots.txt for Circulation Studio
{% if deploy.indexable -%}
# Production deploy. Nothing is restricted except internal tools.
User-agent: *
Allow: /
{% for tool in internalOnly %}Disallow: {{ tool }}
{% endfor -%}
{%- else -%}
# NOT the production deploy ({{ deploy.branch or "local build" }}).
# This origin serves the same pages as the production host and must not compete
# with it in an index. Crawling is allowed ON PURPOSE: every page here carries a
# noindex meta tag, and a crawler has to be able to fetch the page to read it.
# Disallow would hide the directive rather than enforce it. Pages here also
# carry a canonical pointing at production. See src/_data/deploy.js.
# Internal tools are Disallowed here too, for the reason in the template.
User-agent: *
Allow: /
{% for tool in internalOnly %}Disallow: {{ tool }}
{% endfor -%}
{%- endif %}
